// A service's OWN use case: the hops inside order-service behind one of its
// requirements, over the containers its model declares.
//
// It lives beside model.likec4, in order-service's own LikeC4 project — never
// in architecture/usecases/, which is read as the fleet map alone and cannot
// resolve a container. The tag names the requirement it keeps, `#req-ORD-PLACE`
// for spec.md's `Requirement-ID: ORD-PLACE`. This model EXTENDS the map, so the
// tag is declared in architecture/landscape.likec4's one specification block,
// beside the fleet's own tags — a second declaration here would be a duplicate.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// What it is FOR, beyond its own grade: it is the inside of a fleet hop.
// architecture/usecases/order-notification.likec4 draws
// `marketplace.orderService -> kafka.orderEvents 'announces OrderPlaced'`, and
// the edge behind that hop publishes `order.OrderPlaced`. ORD-PLACE's
// `Publishes: order.OrderPlaced` line holds the same name, and this flow claims
// ORD-PLACE — so `loam delta <FEAT> --service order-service --json` lists this
// view under that hop's `inside`, with `via: ["ORD-PLACE"]`. Nothing links the
// two documents but the name both already carry; the join is read, not stored.
// Delete this file and the hop's inside is `joined` with no flows — a fact the
// brief states, never a finding.

views {
  dynamic view order_service_uc_place_order {
    #req-ORD-PLACE
    title 'Place an order'
    description 'An accepted order is written with its outbox row, and the relay publishes OrderPlaced once committed'

    marketplace.orderService.api -> marketplace.orderService.domain 'places the order'
    marketplace.orderService.domain -> marketplace.orderService.db 'writes the order and its outbox row in one transaction'
    marketplace.orderService.outbox -> marketplace.orderService.db 'polls the committed outbox row'
    marketplace.orderService.outbox -> kafka.orderEvents 'publishes OrderPlaced'
  }
}
